    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1493">

                <pb facs="1198" n="1082" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg1082"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 817.51/297A.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                            various Senators</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Circular letter.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-01-22">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">January 22, 1912</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Senator</hi>: I have ventured to send you
                    copy of an address I recently made on “The Monroe Doctrine and some incidental
                    obligations in the zone of the Caribbean.” I do so in the hope of increasing
                    your interest in the Convention between the United States and Honduras, and even
                    more especially the Convention between the United States and Nicaragua, which
                    now await the advice and consent of the Senate. As you know, these Conventions
                    are urged as a practical measure of peace; as a real benefit to commerce,
                    particularly <pb facs="1199" n="1083" xml:id="pg1083"/> of the Southern States;
                    and as a safe and provident measure of foreign policy. Moreover, the
                    regeneration of Nicaragua clearly depends upon our willingness now to extend a
                    helping hand.</p>
                <p>Trusting that this important subject may have the advantage of your impartial
                    consideration,</p>
                <p>I remain [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">P. C. Knox.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>


                <xi:include href="d1493-a1.xml" xpointer="d1493-a1"/>
            </div>
